// Amplitude amplification — the quantum core of amplitude estimation
// (the technique JPMorgan/IBM used to price options with 100x fewer
// samples than classical Monte Carlo). A = RY(theta) prepares |1> with
// probability sin^2(theta/2) — stand-in for whatever you're estimating.
// Each application of Q rotates that amplitude a further 2*theta, same
// mechanism as Grover's, same quadratic speedup.
//
// Real Iterative Amplitude Estimation adds an adaptive classical loop on
// top (grow the number of Q rounds, narrow a confidence interval on
// theta from the stats) — not shown here, this is just the quantum
// primitive it's built on, run for a fixed number of rounds.

PARAM theta : angle
qubit q

RY theta q

// "RY -theta q" doesn't parse (no negative-angle literal for a PARAM),
// so RY(-theta) is built as X . RY(theta) . X instead — checked this
// against the simulator, RY(theta) then that sandwich returns to |0>.
GATE amplify q {
Z q         // Schi: phase-flip the |1> ("good") outcome
X q
RY theta q
X q         // A(inverse) = RY(-theta), via the X-sandwich identity
X q
Z q
X q         // S0: reflect about |0>
RY theta q  // A: re-prepare
}

// Two rounds — each extra `AMPLIFY q` line compounds the effect fast.
AMPLIFY q
AMPLIFY q

MEASURE

// quell run amplitude_estimation.quell --param theta=0.6
// At theta=0.6: baseline P(|1>) ~10%, one round ~59%, two rounds ~99%.
// Matches the predicted sin²((2k+1)θ/2) at each step.
